---
import DocLayout from '../../layouts/DocLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../config/site';
import { PT_STATE_PAGES } from '../../content/professional-tax';
import { STATES } from '../../lib/tax/professional-tax';

const detailed = new Set(PT_STATE_PAGES.map((p) => p.code));
const verified = STATES.filter((s) => s.pt === 'verified');
const none = STATES.filter((s) => s.pt === 'not-levied');
const unverified = STATES.filter((s) => s.pt === 'unverified');
---

<DocLayout
  title={`Professional tax by state (${SITE.shortYearLabel})`}
  description="Which Indian states charge professional tax, how much, and which charge nothing in 2026-27. Includes the February rule in Maharashtra and Karnataka, West Bengal's mid-year change, Odisha's repeal and Bihar's September deduction."
  updated="2026-10-04"
  ogImage="professional-tax"
  about={['Professional tax']}
>
  <p>
    Professional tax is a state levy on employment, deducted from your salary by your employer. The
    Constitution caps it at <strong>&#8377;2,500 a year</strong>, but the slabs, the exemptions and
    even the name differ from state to state.
  </p>
  <p>A few things catch most calculators out:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>February is different</strong> in Maharashtra and Karnataka, where the monthly amount
      rises from &#8377;200 to &#8377;300 so the year lands exactly on &#8377;2,500.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>West Bengal changed its slabs mid-year</strong>, on 1 October 2026, so 2026-27 has two
      different tables.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Punjab charges &#8377;200 a month</strong> under a differently named Act, and is often
      wrongly listed as a state with no professional tax.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Odisha abolished professional tax</strong> from 1 April 2026, by an Ordinance
      published on 21 April 2026.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The calendar differs.</strong> Bihar takes the whole year's tax from September's
      salary, Jharkhand deducts quarterly, Kerala and Chennai half-yearly, and Madhya Pradesh adds a
      few rupees to the twelfth month.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>States we calculate in full</h2>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>State</th>
          <th>Details</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {verified.map((s) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{s.name}</td>
            <td>
              {detailed.has(s.code) ? (
                <a href={`/professional-tax/${s.code.toLowerCase()}`}>Slabs and examples</a>
              ) : (
                'Calculated in the tool'
              )}
            </td>
          </tr>
        ))}
        <tr>
          <td>Tamil Nadu (Chennai only)</td>
          <td>
            <a href="/professional-tax/tn">Slabs and examples</a>
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>States and union territories that charge nothing</h2>
  <p>If you work in one of these, no professional tax is deducted from your salary at all:</p>
  <p>{none.map((s) => s.name).join(', ')}.</p>
  <p>
    Himachal Pradesh is on that list deliberately. It is sometimes reported as having introduced
    professional tax, but the only instrument is a 2005 Bill whose levy was withdrawn that November.
    Odisha joined the list on 1 April 2026; <a href="/professional-tax/or">what the repeal means</a>
    .
  </p>

  <h2>States we do not calculate</h2>
  <p>
    These states levy professional tax, but we have not verified their current slabs against a
    primary source, so the calculator shows zero and says so rather than guessing. You can enter the
    figure from your payslip in advanced mode:
  </p>
  <p>{unverified.map((s) => s.name).join(', ')}.</p>
  <p>
    Tamil Nadu is the awkward one: each corporation, municipality and panchayat sets its own
    half-yearly rates, and the state publishes no single table. We calculate Greater Chennai
    Corporation's rates when you choose Chennai as your city. Kerala, by contrast, uses one
    statewide table, so it is calculated everywhere.
  </p>

  <h2>Does professional tax reduce your income tax?</h2>
  <p>
    In the old regime, yes: it is deducted from taxable salary. In the new regime it is not
    deductible at all, because section 202(2) of the Income-tax Act, 2025 removes that deduction.
    The <a href="/">calculator</a> applies this correctly in each regime.
  </p>
</DocLayout>
